 I'm  going to take that opportunity I have a  clicker I don't have a clicker you say a  few words about okay I think the magic  formula for success of the business any  business is people in culture people say  what did you do to be successful how did  you construct portfolios how did you get  the deep knowledge I think the most  important ingredients are people and and  culture culture is values and the living  those values out in a way every day  so people so what do I mean by great  people I think the most important  qualities of people are three qualities  are character common sense and  creativity and the most important  quality is values you know we interview  people most companies look for skills  there are three things I think that make  up an individual there are their values  those are the things that draw people  together over a people over a period of  time whether you have a happy marriage  or though the best friends that you're  ever going to have or the people are  going to have the same values those are  the core most important ingredients then  there are abilities  natural abilities the ability to do math  or some people are naturally talents in  art we're given a certain ability  certain set of abilities and skills  skills are the things that I think most  companies make the mistake of putting  number one they'll ask different people  what are your skills did you do that job  before but they'll never ask them  questions like why do you work why do  you work for example yeah when we hum  when we hire people the most important  question is values well why do you work  if you're interested in working for  money you don't belong at Bridgewater if  you're interested in working for money  and status you don't belong at  Bridgewater we want people who live out  this culture  in which we deal with create a community  so this community these are the words  that are essential to the process we  have to have group values if you don't  talk about the values and the people  that you're working with how do you know  you have those values so when I look at  we've been doing this now for 35 years  35 years is a testament to the character  of the people who are doing that work so  35 years  you can tell what types of qualities  they have if they're doing it for money  there's a time where money there's a  choice between money and excellence and  you have to I think have those kinds of  values those deep values so you have to  have those group values one of the most  overriding value for us is truth and we  talk about the debate to get a truth so  you hit might hear that Bridgewater is a  very tough place to be and those that  truth through this debate process is the  thing that makes us better and that  truth sometimes has to include things  like things that you're not good at what  your weaknesses are I don't think that  there's a problem if people have  weaknesses and they know they have  weaknesses the greatest barrier that  people have in their own personal  evolution is not understanding their  weaknesses and running into them all the  time so we have a process which is a non  egotistical process of trying to get at  the truth  we have radical transparency to get at  the truth for example you can't talk  about anybody without that person being  in the room they're almost all of our  meetings are taped so anybody in the  company can listen to them through that  radical transparency because that's  produces an idea meritocracy and  excellence that's that's what we try for  that's excellence is not about money  Stradivarius built beautiful violins not  because he thought that they would be  more profitable to sell an expensive  Stradivarius violin there's a sense of  beauty and the people in  operation have to have that sense of  beauty the sense of beauty in terms of  the investment process the sense of  beauty in terms of every aspect client  service every aspect has to be that and  we appreciate each other and we fight 